course: language
lesson: 51
title: Just a scratch and I'm fine: when to worry and tell an adult
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes playing down is dangerous. A friend who is hurt, scared or in trouble may say «It's just a scratch», «I'm fine, honestly», «It's nothing», because they are embarrassed, don't want a fuss, or don't want to get into trouble.
explain: Picture Mia after falling off her scooter: her knee keeps bleeding through a tissue and she says «It's just a scratch, I'm fine, don't tell my mum». Her words are small, but the blood is the evidence, and the wise friend gets an adult anyway.
explain: Grown-ups play things down too, like Grandad saying «it's nothing, I'm just a bit puffed» when he is pale on the stairs. And strangers online use «no biggie» to make a big thing sound small.
explain: But not every «I'm fine» is a secret problem: a paper cut with one drop of blood really is just a scratch. The wise reader checks the evidence both ways, and when in doubt, tells a trusted adult — that is never telling tales.
fact: People often play down a real injury or problem because they are embarrassed, scared of getting into trouble, or don't want a fuss.
fact: Blood that keeps coming through a tissue means more than a scratch; get an adult, even if your friend asks you not to.
fact: Feeling dizzy or sick after a hard bang on the head means tell an adult straight away, always; in Ireland «I'm grand» means «I'm fine», and saying it twice does not make it truer.
fact: If a friend is quiet, eats alone and doesn't want to play for weeks, «I'm fine, it's nothing» may not be true; be kind, stay near, and tell a teacher you trust.
fact: When a stranger online says «it's no biggie, just tell me what school you go to», it IS a big deal: don't answer, block, and tell a grown-up you trust.
fact: Banter is joking that is fun for everyone; if one person keeps getting hurt day after day, it is not banter, it can be bullying, and a teacher should be told.
fact: Grown-ups play things down too: if Grandad is pale and has to stop for breath on the stairs, tell another grown-up what you saw.
fact: With a nut allergy, tingly lips after eating can be the start of a reaction that gets dangerous fast; tell an adult straight away.
fact: A swelling ankle may be sprained; running on it can make it worse, so tell the coach.
fact: For a burn, cool water for about twenty minutes and a grown-up straight away; repeating «I'm fine, I'm fine» fast often means «I'm not».
fact: «Money's a bit tight this month, kiddo» means there is less money than usual; it is a real worry for grown-ups but not a reason to panic, and it is not a child's job to fix.
fact: Once is nothing, every day is a pattern: a friend skipping lunch once is fine, but every day this week is worth telling a trusted teacher.
fact: Sometimes the words match the evidence: a tiny paper cut with one drop of blood really is just a scratch, and making a huge fuss is not needed.
exercise: Mia's knee keeps bleeding through a tissue. «It's just a scratch, don't tell my mum». What should you do? | Get an adult, even though she's embarrassed | Keep her secret, because she asked you to and you promised | Give her another tissue and go on playing | Tell her to wash it in the pond on the way
exercise: Conor bangs his head hard and now feels dizzy and sick. «Ah I'm grand, honestly, I'm grand». What should you do? | Tell an adult straight away | Believe him, he said grand twice | Let him rest on the frame | Tell him to shake it off
exercise: What does «grand» mean in Ireland? | Fine | Very big | Very rich | Very old
exercise: For weeks Amara has been quiet, eats alone and says «I'm fine. Whatever. It's nothing». What is the kind thing to do? | Stay near her and tell a teacher you trust | Leave her alone because she said it's nothing | Tell the whole class that Amara is sad | Ask her the same question every five minutes
exercise: A stranger in a game messages: «its no biggie, just tell me what school u go to, everyone does it». What should you do? | Don't answer, block, and tell a trusted grown-up | Tell them, because everyone does it | Tell them the name of a different school | Answer only if they seem friendly and have a nice picture
exercise: A group throws Ben's hat in the bin every day and says «Chill, it's just banter». What is it really? | It can be bullying, because Ben keeps getting hurt | It's banter, because they say they're only messing about | It's a game that Ben should learn to laugh at | It's fine as long as the hat isn't broken
exercise: What is real banter? | Joking that's fun for everyone | Joking that's fun for the group | Joking that one person hates | Joking that happens every day
exercise: Grandad is pale and stops on the stairs to catch his breath: «Ah, it's nothing, I'm just a bit puffed». What should you do? | Tell another grown-up what you saw | Believe him, because grown-ups know | Race him to the top of the stairs | Tell him to stop moaning
exercise: Noah has a nut allergy, eats a cookie and says «My lips are a bit tingly, it's nothing». What should you do? | Tell an adult straight away | Carry on playing, it's only a bit | Give him another cookie to check | Tell him to drink some lemonade
exercise: Yusuf's ankle is swelling after he went over on it: «It's just a knock, I'll run it off». What should happen? | Tell the coach, and he should stop | Let him run on, because it's only a knock | Tie his laces tighter and play on | Swap boots with him and carry on
exercise: Freya's hand is red from a hot pan: «it's only a little burn, I'm fine I'm fine». What is the right first help? | Cool water for about twenty minutes and a grown-up | Butter on the burn and then carry on cooking | Nothing, because she said she's fine twice | A plaster stuck straight on top of the burn, pressed down hard
exercise: Mum at the bills: «Money's a bit tight this month, kiddo». What does it mean? | There is less money than usual this month | The family has no money at all, ever again | Money is folded too tightly in her purse | It means nothing, so ask for an expensive game
exercise: Kemi has skipped lunch every day this week: «I'm not hungry, no biggie». What should Lucy do? | Tell a teacher she trusts | Eat Kemi's lunch for her each day | Believe her every single time she says it | Tell Kemi to stop being silly
exercise: Oscar gets a paper cut with one drop of blood: «Ow. It's just a scratch, I'm fine». What is true? | He really is fine | He needs an ambulance | He is hiding a big injury | He should go home at once
exercise: Why do people sometimes play down a real injury? | They are embarrassed or scared of trouble | They want everyone to make a big fuss | They have forgotten how to talk about being hurt | They want to get a day off school
exercise: Is telling a trusted adult that a friend is hurt the same as telling tales? | No, it is keeping your friend safe | Yes, and you should never do it | Yes, unless the friend says it's okay | No, because tales are only about animals
test: «she said don't tell her mum so I didn't, it's still bleeding tho» What should you do now? | Tell an adult now: a promise doesn't count when someone's hurt | Keep quiet, because a promise is a promise | Wait until tomorrow and see if it stops bleeding by itself in the night | Tell her to hide it under her trousers
test: «Conor said he's grand so we carried on playing, he's just being a bit sick lol» What is wrong? | Being sick after a head bang means an adult must know at once | Nothing, «grand» means he's fine to keep playing, so the game goes on | Nothing, being sick is normal in a game | He should drink water and play in goal
test: ★ «this guy online said everyone tells him their school so it's no biggie» Why is «everyone does it» a warning sign here? | A stranger is making a big private thing sound small | It shows he is a famous gamer that everyone can trust | It means the school has said it's okay | It is a warning sign only if he's rude
test: «it's just banter, Ben, why are you so sensitive?» Ben's hat goes in the bin for the fifth day. What is right? | If one person keeps getting hurt, it isn't banter | Ben should learn to laugh at it like all the others do | It's banter as long as the group is laughing | It's fine, because hats can be washed
test: ★ Grandad says «it's nothing», and he's a grown-up. Why still tell someone? | Grown-ups play things down too, and a doctor may need to check | Because grown-ups never know when they are ill, so children must decide | Because Grandad is always lying about everything | Because you want him to get into trouble
test: ★ What would happen if Noah's friends believed «a bit tingly, it's nothing»? | A reaction could get dangerous before an adult knew | Nothing at all, because tingly lips are always fine | His lips would stop tingling the next day | He would get better faster by playing
test: «Mum says money's tight, so we're gonna be homeless, aren't we??» What is the best answer? | It's a real worry for grown-ups but not a reason to panic; you can ask Mum about it | Yes, «tight» always means you will lose your house tomorrow | No, it means nothing, so ask for that new game | You must find a job and pay the bills yourself
test: ★★ Kemi said «no biggie» on Monday and Lucy believed her. By Friday she has skipped lunch five days running. What has changed? | Once was nothing, but every day is a pattern to tell a teacher about | Nothing, because Kemi said «no biggie» on the first day | Kemi has decided to stop eating lunch for a diet | Lucy should eat with someone else from now on
test: ★★ Oscar: paper cut, one drop of blood, «it's just a scratch». Mia: knee bleeding through a tissue, «it's just a scratch». What is the difference? | For Oscar the words match what you see; for Mia they don't | There is no difference, because both said the same words | Mia is fine and Oscar needs a doctor | Both need an ambulance straight away
test: ★★★ Freya says «I'm fine» once, calmly, with dry eyes. Later she says «I'm fine I'm fine I'm fine» fast, holding back tears. Which tells you more that she needs help? | The fast, repeated «I'm fine» with tears | The calm «I'm fine» with dry eyes | Neither, because she said fine each time | Both mean exactly the same thing
test: ★★★ Yusuf's ankle swells. Coach is 50 metres away; the match ends in 30 minutes. Yusuf: «I'll run it off, it's just a knock». What is the best plan? | Tell the coach now, not after the match | Let him play the last 30 minutes, then tell | Run 50 metres with him to the coach | Wait to see if the swelling gets bigger first
test: ★★★ Which of these is NOT a reason to worry and tell an adult? | A tiny paper cut with one drop of blood | Tingly lips after a cookie, with a nut allergy | Dizziness after a bang on the head | A stranger asking for your school
exam: Identify the appropriate course of action where a child, whose knee continues to bleed through a tissue, insists that the wound is «just a scratch» and requests secrecy. | An adult is to be informed notwithstanding the request | The request for secrecy is to be honoured without exception | The child is to be advised to continue playing | The wound is to be concealed beneath clothing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an assertion by an unknown person online that disclosing one's school is «no biggie»? | It constitutes a warning sign; no reply should be made and an adult informed | It constitutes reassurance that the request is harmless | It constitutes an obligation to reply promptly | It constitutes a customary greeting among players
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning conduct described as «banter» which repeatedly causes distress to one individual. | It is not banter and may constitute bullying | It is banter, provided the group is amused | It is banter, provided no property is destroyed | It is banter, provided it occurs in the lavatories
exam: It is given that a pupil sustains a paper cut yielding a single drop of blood and declares himself «fine». Ascertain the correct interpretation. | The words correspond to the evidence, and no fuss is required | The words conceal a grave injury requiring an ambulance | The pupil is to be sent home forthwith | All such declarations are to be disbelieved
